tools.description:
Creates one or more Notion pages with the specified properties and content.

## Core rules

**IMPORTANT**: Before writing page content, read the MCP resource `notion://docs/notion-flavored-markdown-spec` through your MCP client's resource-reading interface, or call the Notion "fetch" tool with this URI if your client does not support reading MCP resources. Do NOT pass this URI to any other URL-fetching tool. Do NOT invent Markdown syntax. Put the page title in properties, not content. By default, use native Notion mentions for references you add to existing Notion pages, databases, data sources, people, and dates. Use Markdown links only for external URLs or when the user requests a plain link. For a person, use a user mention with their user URL, found with search using query_type "user"; do not write "@Name" as plain text. For a specific date or time, such as a due date or meeting time, use a date mention. The Markdown specification gives the mention syntax.

Use a named destination as parent. Otherwise use "creation_mode": "draft" for a durable private page. Do not combine draft mode with parent, or move or share it without direction.

For a meeting note, use create_meeting_note if available, even in a Meetings database, unless the user wants a regular page or a template.

For a database, ALWAYS fetch first. Use its collection:// URL as data_source_id, exact property names, and title property. Never put a database ID in page_id. Outside a database, the only allowed property is the required "title".

For a template, set "as_template": true.

For reusable instructions, set "is_skill": true. Before creating a skill, read the MCP resource `notion://docs/skills` through your MCP client's resource-reading interface. If your client does not support reading MCP resources, call the Notion "fetch" tool with this URI instead. Do NOT pass this URI to any other URL-fetching tool. Do not mark ordinary pages or drafts as skills. Report any "warnings" in the result to the user.

After a successful create (for async_task, once it succeeds), show one new page by passing its id or url as page_id to notion-page-panel. Skip this for memory saves or when the user wants only a link.

```ts
mcp__Notion__notion_create_pages(args: {
// The pages to create.
// maxItems: 100
pages: Array<
{
// The properties of the new page, which is a JSON map of property names to SQLite values.
// For pages in a database, use the SQLite schema definition shown in <database>.
// For pages outside of a database, the only allowed property is "title", which is the title of the page and is automatically shown at the top of the page as a large heading.
// For a Files property, an uploaded file can be provided as {"type":"file_upload","file_upload":{"id":"<file-upload-id>"}} inside the property's array.
// **IMPORTANT**: Some property types require specific formats:
// - Date properties: Split into "date:{property}:start", "date:{property}:end" (optional), and "date:{property}:is_datetime" (0 or 1)
// - Place properties: Split into "place:{property}:name", "place:{property}:address", "place:{property}:latitude", "place:{property}:longitude", and "place:{property}:google_place_id" (optional)
// - Number properties: Use JavaScript numbers (not strings)
// - Checkbox properties: Use "__YES__" for checked, "__NO__" for unchecked
// - Relation properties: Use an array of related page URLs or page IDs, e.g. ["https://www.notion.so/26ab1f9f4c5f80b18d3bd10a6b1d2f4e", "26ab1f9f-4c5f-80b1-8d3b-d10a6b1d2f4e"]
// - Person properties: Use an array of user IDs, user or agent URLs, or group references copied from fetch output ("space_permission_group-<UUID>"). Bare group UUIDs are also supported.
// - Files properties: Use a JSON array of file IDs, Notion Folder URLs, and/or <folder> tags copied from fetch output. Folders are stored as native Folder references, not ordinary links.
//
//
// **Special property naming**: Properties named "id" or "url" (case insensitive) must be prefixed with "userDefined:" (e.g., "userDefined:URL", "userDefined:id")
properties?: {
[key: string]:
 | string
 | number
// maxItems: 100
 | Array<

 | string
 | {
// Always `file_upload`
type: "file_upload",
// The uploaded file to attach to the Files property.
file_upload: {
// ID of a file upload returned by create_file_upload or create_attachment.
id: string,
[key: string]: any,
},
[key: string]: any,
}

>
 | null
,
},
// The content of the new page, using Notion Markdown. For people and dates, use user and date mentions rather than plain text; notion://docs/notion-flavored-markdown-spec gives the syntax.
content?: string,
// The ID of a template to apply to this page. When specified, do not provide 'content' as the template will provide it. Properties can still be set alongside the template. Get template IDs from the <templates> section in the fetch tool results, or from the end of a page_templates URL. Template application is asynchronous: the page starts blank and content appears shortly after.
template_id?: string,
// Set to true to create a reusable database template instead of an ordinary page. Requires a data_source_id or database_id parent and edit access to the data source. Properties and content define the template. Cannot be combined with template_id. Defaults to false; does not change the default template or configure repetition.
as_template?: boolean,
// An emoji character (e.g. "🚀"), a custom emoji by name (e.g. ":rocket_ship:"), a Notion icon identifier as returned by fetch (e.g. "icons/pizza_blue"), or an external image URL. Use "none" to explicitly set no icon. Omit to leave unchanged. Keep the title free of a duplicate leading emoji because the icon renders separately.
icon?: string,
// An external image URL for the page cover. Use "none" to explicitly set no cover. Omit to leave unchanged.
cover?: string,
// Set to true for a full-width page, or false for the default width. Omit to use the default. For database pages, overrides the database layout for this page only.
full_width?: boolean,
// Set to true only when the user asks for reusable instructions or a repeatable workflow. Do not mark ordinary reference pages, one-time documents, or drafts as skills. Before creating a skill, read the MCP resource `notion://docs/skills` through your MCP client's resource-reading interface. If your client does not support reading MCP resources, call the Notion "fetch" tool with this URI instead. Do NOT pass this URI to any other URL-fetching tool. False has the same effect as omitting the field.
is_skill?: boolean,
// Preserve internal Markdown links instead of converting them to native mentions.
preserve_internal_links?: boolean,
}
>,
// If the user explicitly names a private or shared destination, omit "creation_mode" and use that parent. Otherwise, use "draft" when the user clearly wants a durable page but has not named a destination. Prefer this explicit draft mode over omitting the parent. Draft mode is server-enforced: it creates a private workspace-level page and cannot be combined with "parent". Create it without first asking where it should live. After creation, tell the user it is private and offer to move it once they name a destination.
creation_mode?: "draft",
// All pages in one call share this parent. Pass "memory" to save a personal memory; see notion://docs/memory. Otherwise use a page (page_id), database page (database_id), data source (data_source_id), or teamspace root (teamspace_id, from get_teams). Teamspace roots require a user-backed connection and permission to add top-level pages. A database_id cannot be used when the database has more than one data source; fetch it and use the right collection:// URL as data_source_id. If omitted, pages are private at the workspace level. When no destination is named, prefer explicit "creation_mode": "draft" over omitting the parent.
parent?:
// Always `memory`
 | ("memory")
 | {
// The ID of the parent page (with or without dashes), for example, 195de9221179449fab8075a27c979105
page_id: string,
// Always `page_id`
type?: "page_id",
[key: string]: any,
}
 | {
// The ID of the parent database (with or without dashes), for example, 195de9221179449fab8075a27c979105
database_id: string,
// Always `database_id`
type?: "database_id",
[key: string]: any,
}
 | {
// The ID of the parent data source (collection), with or without dashes. For example, f336d0bc-b841-465b-8045-024475c079dd
data_source_id: string,
// Always `data_source_id`
type?: "data_source_id",
[key: string]: any,
}
 | {
// The ID of the teamspace in which to create top-level pages (with or without dashes).
teamspace_id: string,
}
,
// Where the pages go in a page parent's content. Omit to add them at the end. {"type":"start"} and {"type":"end"} add them at the top or bottom of the page. {"type":"after_content","anchor":"..."} adds them after the block where the anchor ends, and {"type":"before_content","anchor":"..."} adds them before the block where the anchor starts. Pages keep their order. Only for a page_id parent.
position?:
 | {
// Insert the content at the start of the page.
type: "start",
[key: string]: any,
}
 | {
// Insert the content at the end of the page.
type: "end",
[key: string]: any,
}
 | {
// Insert the content after the block where the anchor ends, at that block's nesting level, so anchoring on a parent block inserts after its children.
type: "after_content",
// Text copied exactly from the page, starting at the beginning of a line with its leading syntax, such as `## ` or `- `, and keeping its inline formatting and escapes. It doesn't need the whole line, only enough to match exactly one place on the page. If the target's text repeats, extend the anchor without moving its end for after_content or its start for before_content: start after_content at an earlier unique line, and continue before_content into the lines after it.
anchor: string,
[key: string]: any,
}
 | {
// Insert the content before the block where the anchor starts, at that block's nesting level.
type: "before_content",
// Text copied exactly from the page, starting at the beginning of a line with its leading syntax, such as `## ` or `- `, and keeping its inline formatting and escapes. It doesn't need the whole line, only enough to match exactly one place on the page. If the target's text repeats, extend the anchor without moving its end for after_content or its start for before_content: start after_content at an earlier unique line, and continue before_content into the lines after it.
anchor: string,
[key: string]: any,
}
,
// Default to true for page creation. Set to false only when the next step needs the created pages immediately, or when async execution rejects the request as too large. When this create operation is accepted for background execution, it returns an async_task result. Use get_async_task to wait for a succeeded status before taking a dependent action on the pages. For pages created with template_id, a succeeded status does not mean template content is ready; fetch and retry until it is ready before changing or relying on that content. If omitted or false, the tool keeps the existing synchronous result shape.
allow_async?: boolean,
// Notion setup instructions version, such as 0.0.2. Omit if unknown.
instructions_version?: string,
// For tracking only: the name of the agent skill you are following for this task, such as notion-meeting-prep. Omit if you are not following a skill.
skill?: string,
[key: string]: any,
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Propose one precise, reviewable change without changing accepted page content. Copy old_text exactly from fetch, use one suggestion per localized change, and use get_suggested_edit to inspect it before any later resolution.

Requires a Business or Enterprise workspace plan. Call this tool directly. If access is denied, follow the recovery guidance in the result.

```ts
mcp__Notion__notion_create_suggested_edit(args: {
// Optional inline Markdown comment that starts the suggestion thread.
comment?: string,
// Notion setup instructions version, such as 0.0.2. Omit if unknown.
instructions_version?: string,
// How to apply new_text relative to old_text. Defaults to replace.
mode?: "replace" | "insert_before" | "insert_after",
// Proposed Notion Markdown. With replace, it replaces old_text; use an empty string to suggest deletion. With insert_before or insert_after, it is only the text to insert and old_text remains unchanged.
new_text: string,
// Unique, non-empty exact text copied from the accepted Notion Markdown returned by fetch.
old_text: string,
// Page UUID or URL containing the target text.
page_id: string,
// Exact page title or database title/text property name. Omit for page body text.
property_name?: string,
// For tracking only: the name of the agent skill you are following for this task, such as notion-meeting-prep. Omit if you are not following a skill.
skill?: string,
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Create a new view on a Notion database.

Use fetch first to get database_id, parent_page_id, and the collection:// data_source_id. The caller needs edit access to the database or parent page and access to the data source.

Provide exactly one placement: database_id adds a view tab to that database; parent_page_id appends an inline linked database view to that page. Always provide data_source_id.

Supported types: table, board, list, calendar, timeline, gallery, form, chart, map, and dashboard.

The optional configure field uses the view DSL for filters, sorts, grouping, and display. Read notion://docs/view-dsl-spec through the MCP resource interface, or pass that URI to the Notion fetch tool. Do not guess the syntax.

```ts
mcp__Notion__notion_create_view(args: {
// View configuration DSL directives and examples:
// - FILTER "Property" = "value" — filter rows. Relation values must be a page URL or UUID; person values must be a user URI (user://<user_id>), user UUID, or "me". Names are not supported for either.
// - QUICK FILTER "Property" — add a quick filter to the view's filter bar with no criteria; add a condition to preselect one (QUICK FILTER "Status" = "Done")
// - SORT BY "Property" ASC — sort rows
// - GROUP BY "Property" [BY OPTION | BY GROUP] [SHOW EMPTY | HIDE EMPTY] — group by property (required for board views)
// - SUB GROUP BY "Property" [BY OPTION | BY GROUP] [SHOW EMPTY | HIDE EMPTY] — add board sub-grouping BY OPTION and BY GROUP apply only to status properties and rollups that return status values (Show original or Show unique). For select and multi_select properties, omit both modifiers, even if the property is named "Status" (for example, GROUP BY "Status").
// - CARD LAYOUT default | compact — set board or gallery card layout
// - CARD SIZE small|medium|large — set gallery card size independently of its cover
// - CALENDAR BY "Property" — date property (required for calendar views)
// - TIMELINE BY "Start" TO "End" — date range (required for timeline views)
// - MAP BY "Property" — location property (required for map views)
// - CHART column|bar|line|donut|number — chart type with optional AGGREGATE, COLOR, HEIGHT, SORT, STACK BY, CAPTION
// - FORM CLOSE|OPEN — close/open form submissions
// - FORM ANONYMOUS true|false — toggle anonymous submissions
// - FORM PERMISSIONS none|reader|editor — set submission permissions
// - SHOW "Prop1", "Prop2" — set visible properties
// - WIDTH "Prop1" 320, "Prop2" 120 — set table column widths in pixels
// - COVER "Property" — cover image property
// - WRAP PROPERTIES true|false — wrap configured gallery property values
// - ICON "🚀" — view tab icon: an emoji, a Notion icon such as "icons/rocket_blue", or an image URL
// - SUBSET FILTER "Property" = "value"; SUBSET LIMIT 10000 — choose which XL database rows this view syncs (XL data sources only)
//
//
// <example>SHOW "Name", "Status", "Due Date"</example>
// <example>GROUP BY "Status" BY GROUP SHOW EMPTY; SUB GROUP BY "Priority"; CARD LAYOUT compact</example>
// <example>SHOW "Name", "Status"; CARD SIZE small; WRAP PROPERTIES true</example>
// <example>FILTER "Status" = "In Progress"; SORT BY "Due Date" ASC</example>
// <example>CALENDAR BY "Due Date"</example>
// <example>TIMELINE BY "Start" TO "End"</example>
// <example>FILTER "Company" = "Acme"</example>
configure?: string,
// The data source (collection) ID. Accepts a collection:// URI from <data-source> tags or a bare UUID.
data_source_id: string,
// The database to add a view tab to. Accepts a Notion URL or a bare UUID. Mutually exclusive with `parent_page_id`; exactly one must be provided.
database_id?: string,
// Notion setup instructions version, such as 0.0.2. Omit if unknown.
instructions_version?: string,
// The name of the view.
name: string,
// A page to create an inline linked database view on, like the UI /linked command. Accepts a Notion URL or a bare UUID. The new linked view block is appended at the end of the page and references `data_source_id`. Mutually exclusive with `database_id`; exactly one must be provided.
parent_page_id?: string,
// Whether the new linked view block shows its data source title above the view, like the Show data source title toggle in the Notion app. Defaults to true. Only valid with `parent_page_id`.
show_data_source_title?: boolean,
// For tracking only: the name of the agent skill you are following for this task, such as notion-meeting-prep. Omit if you are not following a skill.
skill?: string,
// The type of view to create.
type: "table" | "board" | "list" | "calendar" | "timeline" | "gallery" | "form" | "chart" | "map" | "dashboard",
[key: string]: any,
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Delete a comment you wrote. Get the comment ID from get_comments or create_comment. If it is the last comment in its discussion, the discussion is removed too. Use this only when the user asks to delete a comment; to fix a comment, use update_comment instead. You can only delete your own comments.

```ts
mcp__Notion__notion_delete_comment(args: {
// The comment ID, with or without dashes. Use the id of a <comment> from get_comments, or the id returned by create_comment.
comment_id: string,
// Notion setup instructions version, such as 0.0.2. Omit if unknown.
instructions_version?: string,
// For tracking only: the name of the agent skill you are following for this task, such as notion-meeting-prep. Omit if you are not following a skill.
skill?: string,
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Move Notion databases to Trash. They can be restored with the restore_pages tool or from Trash in Notion. Each database goes to Trash with all of its rows. Pass a full-page or inline database that owns its data, not a linked view. To move a page or database row to Trash, use delete_pages. Use this tool only when the user explicitly asks to delete specific databases. Never use it on your own initiative to clean up, deduplicate, or reorganize content. Without user_confirmed: true, nothing is trashed and the error lists what would be moved to Trash; show that list to the user and retry only after they confirm.

```ts
mcp__Notion__notion_delete_databases(args: {
// IDs of the databases to move to Trash, 1 to 20. Each must be a full-page or inline database that owns its data, not a linked view. Duplicate IDs are processed once.
database_ids: string[], // minItems: 1, maxItems: 20
// Notion setup instructions version, such as 0.0.2. Omit if unknown.
instructions_version?: string,
// For tracking only: the name of the agent skill you are following for this task, such as notion-meeting-prep. Omit if you are not following a skill.
skill?: string,
// Set to true only after the user explicitly asked to delete these exact databases, or confirmed after you showed them the list. If omitted or false, nothing is trashed and the error lists what would be moved to Trash so you can confirm with the user.
user_confirmed?: boolean,
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Move Notion pages to Trash. They can be restored with the restore_pages tool or from Trash in Notion. Database rows are pages. To move a database to Trash, use delete_databases. To remove content inside a page, use update_page instead. Use this tool only when the user explicitly asks to delete specific pages. Never use it on your own initiative to clean up, deduplicate, or reorganize content. Without user_confirmed: true, nothing is trashed and the error lists what would be moved to Trash; show that list to the user and retry only after they confirm.

```ts
mcp__Notion__notion_delete_pages(args: {
// Notion setup instructions version, such as 0.0.2. Omit if unknown.
instructions_version?: string,
// IDs of the pages to move to Trash, 1 to 20. Database rows are pages. Duplicate IDs are processed once.
page_ids: string[], // minItems: 1, maxItems: 20
// For tracking only: the name of the agent skill you are following for this task, such as notion-meeting-prep. Omit if you are not following a skill.
skill?: string,
// Set to true only after the user explicitly asked to delete these exact pages, or confirmed after you showed them the list. If omitted or false, nothing is trashed and the error lists what would be moved to Trash so you can confirm with the user.
user_confirmed?: boolean,
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Download the contents of a small UTF-8 text attachment created by the Notion MCP `create-attachment` tool.

Pass the `file_upload_id` returned by `create-attachment`. The attachment must belong to the requesting integration, have completed uploading, and use a supported text format such as HTML, Markdown, plain text, CSV, JSON, XML, CSS, YAML, TSV, calendar, GPX, or SVG.

The response contains the complete text in `content` so you can save it locally, edit it, and call `create-attachment` again to upload a new version. Downloads are limited to 200 KiB. This tool does not fetch arbitrary URLs or return binary files. For larger or binary attachments, use the signed file URL returned when reading the containing Notion page.

<examples>
1. Download a text attachment: {"file_upload_id":"12345678-90ab-cdef-1234-567890abcdef"}
</examples>

```ts
mcp__Notion__notion_download_attachment(args: {
// The FileUpload ID returned by the create-attachment tool.
file_upload_id: string,
// Notion setup instructions version, such as 0.0.2. Omit if unknown.
instructions_version?: string,
// For tracking only: the name of the agent skill you are following for this task, such as notion-meeting-prep. Omit if you are not following a skill.
skill?: string,
[key: string]: any,
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Download a spec-compliant Notion Skill as a complete tar.gz archive containing SKILL.md and its supporting files and nested folders. Pass the skill page ID. Returns a temporary signed url. Download and extract the archive to read or use the skill.

```ts
mcp__Notion__notion_download_skill(args: {
// Identifier for a Notion skill page.
id: string,
// Notion setup instructions version, such as 0.0.2. Omit if unknown.
instructions_version?: string,
// For tracking only: the name of the agent skill you are following for this task, such as notion-meeting-prep. Omit if you are not following a skill.
skill?: string,
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Duplicate a Notion page. The page must be within the current workspace, and you must have permission to access it. The duplication completes asynchronously, so do not rely on the new page identified by the returned ID or URL to be populated immediately. Let the user know that the duplication is in progress and that they can check back later using the 'fetch' tool or by clicking the returned URL and viewing it in the Notion app.

```ts
mcp__Notion__notion_duplicate_page(args: {
// Notion setup instructions version, such as 0.0.2. Omit if unknown.
instructions_version?: string,
// The page ID, with or without dashes, or a Notion page URL.
page_id: string,
// For tracking only: the name of the agent skill you are following for this task, such as notion-meeting-prep. Omit if you are not following a skill.
skill?: string,
[key: string]: any,
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Retrieves the current status of an async task that was started by another tool (for example, "create_pages" called with "allow_async": true).

The status is one of "queued", "running", "retrying", "succeeded", or "failed". When the task has succeeded, the operation's result is included; when it has failed, an error is included instead.

Poll this tool with the "task_id" from the original tool's "async_task" response. Wait briefly between polls — the original response includes a suggested backoff.

<examples>
1. Check a task's status: {"task_id": "task_abc123"}
</examples>

```ts
mcp__Notion__notion_get_async_task(args: {
// Notion setup instructions version, such as 0.0.2. Omit if unknown.
instructions_version?: string,
// For tracking only: the name of the agent skill you are following for this task, such as notion-meeting-prep. Omit if you are not following a skill.
skill?: string,
// The ID of the async task to retrieve, as returned in the async_task response of the tool that started it.
task_id: string, // minLength: 1
[key: string]: any,
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Get comments and discussions from a Notion page.

Returns page-level and inline discussions in XML format. Long results are shortened:
- If `shown-count` is less than `total-count`, some discussions were left out. Up to 50 are listed as `<omitted-discussion id="discussion://..."/>`: pass that URL as `discussion_id` to fetch it. If `omitted-truncated="true"`, more were left out; `fetch` with `include_discussions: true` may show their URLs.
- Each discussion has `comment-count` (comments shown) and `total-comment-count`. A discussion that does not fit keeps its newest comments. When older comments were left out, it has `next-cursor`: pass it as `cursor` with that `discussion_id` to get the next older comments. Repeat until a response has no `next-cursor`. If it has `reactions-truncated="true"`, some of its reactions were left out to fit. On supported Business connections, pending suggested edits use `kind="suggested_edit"` and their `discussion://` URL can be passed to `get_suggested_edit`. Resolved suggestions stay retired. Check `suggested_edits_status` to distinguish an empty result from unavailable suggestion discovery. Comments you wrote have `by-you="true"`.


Tip: Use the `fetch` tool with `include_discussions: true` first to see where discussions are anchored in the page content, then use this tool to retrieve full discussion threads. The `discussion://` URLs in the fetch output match the discussion IDs returned here.

Parameters:
- `include_resolved`: Include resolved discussions (default: false)
- `discussion_id`: Fetch a specific discussion by ID or URL
- `cursor`: A discussion's `next-cursor`, to get its older comments (requires `discussion_id`)


<example>{"page_id": "page-uuid"}</example>
<example>{"page_id": "page-uuid", "discussion_id": "discussion://pageId/blockId/discussionId"}</example>
<example>{"page_id": "page-uuid", "discussion_id": "discussion://pageId/blockId/discussionId", "cursor": "comment-uuid"}</example>

```ts
mcp__Notion__notion_get_comments(args: {
// The `next-cursor` value from a discussion in an earlier response. Returns that discussion's older comments. Requires `discussion_id`.
cursor?: string,
// Fetch a specific discussion by ID or discussion URL (e.g., discussion://pageId/blockId/discussionId).
discussion_id?: string,
// Include resolved discussions in the response. Defaults to false.
include_resolved?: boolean,
// Notion setup instructions version, such as 0.0.2. Omit if unknown.
instructions_version?: string,
// The page ID, with or without dashes, or a Notion page URL.
page_id: string,
// For tracking only: the name of the agent skill you are following for this task, such as notion-meeting-prep. Omit if you are not following a skill.
skill?: string,
[key: string]: any,
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Use this tool to view the Notion skills the user has enabled for you and other external MCP clients, each with a name and a description of when it should be used. If you choose to use one of these skills, mention to the user that you are loading the skill from Notion first, then download them.

```ts
mcp__Notion__notion_get_enabled_skills(args: {
// Notion setup instructions version, such as 0.0.2. Omit if unknown.
instructions_version?: string,
// For tracking only: the name of the agent skill you are following for this task, such as notion-meeting-prep. Omit if you are not following a skill.
skill?: string,
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Get signed download URLs for files that fetch output cites by stable `notion-file-block://` references.

Pass up to 25 references exactly as they appear in the page Markdown. Each reference resolves independently: `files` lists the ones that resolved and `unresolved` lists the rest with a reason. The URLs expire after a few minutes; call this tool again for fresh ones. Keep the stable references, not the URLs, when editing or copying page Markdown.

<examples>
1. Get download URLs: {"references":["notion-file-block://12345678-90ab-4def-8234-567890abcdef/abcdef12-3456-4890-abcd-ef1234567890?space_id=fedcba98-7654-4321-8fed-cba987654321&name=diagram.png"]}
</examples>

```ts
mcp__Notion__notion_get_file_download_urls(args: {
// Notion setup instructions version, such as 0.0.2. Omit if unknown.
instructions_version?: string,
// Stable notion-file-block:// file references, copied exactly from the Markdown returned by fetch. Up to 25 per call.
references: string[], // minItems: 1, maxItems: 25
