Hand an SMS conversation to Sona, Quo's AI agent, to get something done with one real person through back-and-forth texting. Sona texts as the user's business: it writes the first text itself, reads each reply, and keeps replying on its own until the goal is reached, the person replies STOP, someone on the user's team replies in the conversation (which takes over from Sona straight away), or it is canceled. Use this when the user wants Sona to handle a conversation, not just send a text, e.g. 'have Sona confirm Jane's appointment', 'get Mike to pick a delivery day', 'follow up with this lead and answer their questions'. To send one text the user wrote or dictated, or a one-way notice that needs no handling of replies, use send-message instead. Sona already knows the business (its services, prices, hours and policies), so the task only needs what is specific to this conversation. Before starting, make sure the goal and the details it needs are clear; if not, ask targeted questions rather than guessing. Restate what Sona will do and confirm the from number, the person to text and the task with the user: texts cannot be unsent, and Quo may also ask the user to confirm. Every request needs an idempotencyKey; reuse it for every retry of the same task. This returns an agent conversation ID straight away: check it with get-agent-conversation in a few minutes, read the texts with fetch-messages, and stop it with cancel-agent-conversation. Sona's tools are in beta: tell the user so if they ask, or when a result falls short of what they expected. When the user comments on how Sona or this tool performed (for example, Sona misunderstood, missed the goal or sounded unnatural), offer to send it to the Quo team with submit-feedback, including the ID of the Sona run or knowledge interview so the team can find it. Corrections to the business's own details, such as a wrong price or outdated hours, go to ask-knowledge-agent instead.

